  "slug": "short-encoding-of-words",
  "hints": [
    "Which words can share an encoded segment with another word? Look for suffix relationships.",
    "What happens to a suffix relationship if every word is reversed?",
    "In a reversed trie, which terminal nodes represent words that need their own encoded segment?"
  ],
  "title": "Short Encoding of Words Problem",
  "content": {
    "notes": [
      "Words that are exact suffixes of another word can share the same encoded substring and trailing '#'.",
      "Duplicate words only need to be represented once in the shortest reference string."
    ],
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= words.length <= 2000",
      "1 <= words[i].length <= 7",
      "words[i] consists of only lowercase letters."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Single line: JSON array of all arguments, e.g. [arg1, arg2, ...]",
    "outputFormat": "Single line: JSON-serialised return value",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "[[\"time\", \"me\", \"bell\"]]",
        "output": "10",
        "explanation": "A valid encoding would be s = \"time#bell#\" and indices = [0, 2, 5].\nwords[0] = \"time\", the substring of s starting from indices[0] = 0 to the next '#' is underlined in \"time#bell#\"\nwords[1] = \"me\", the substring of s starting from indices[1] = 2 to the next '#' is underlined in \"time#bell#\"\nwords[2] = \"bell\", the substring of s starting from indices[2] = 5 to the next '#' is underlined in \"time#bell#\""
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "[[\"t\"]]",
        "output": "2",
        "explanation": "A valid encoding would be s = \"t#\" and indices = [0]."
      }
    ],
    "problemStatement": "A **valid encoding** of an array of `words` is any reference string `s` and array of indices `indices` such that:\n\n* `words.length == indices.length`\n* The reference string `s` ends with the `'#'` character.\n* For each index `indices[i]`, the **substring** of `s` starting from `indices[i]` and up to (but not including) the next `'#'` character is equal to `words[i]`.\n\nWith an array of `words`, output *the **length of the shortest reference string*** `s` *possible of any **valid encoding** of* `words`*.*\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: words = [\"time\", \"me\", \"bell\"]\nOutput: 10\nExplanation: A valid encoding would be s = \"time#bell#\" and indices = [0, 2, 5].\nwords[0] = \"time\", the substring of s starting from indices[0] = 0 to the next '#' is underlined in \"time#bell#\"\nwords[1] = \"me\", the substring of s starting from indices[1] = 2 to the next '#' is underlined in \"time#bell#\"\nwords[2] = \"bell\", the substring of s starting from indices[2] = 5 to the next '#' is underlined in \"time#bell#\"\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: words = [\"t\"]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: A valid encoding would be s = \"t#\" and indices = [0].\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n* `1 <= words.length <= 2000`\n* `1 <= words[i].length <= 7`\n* `words[i]` is composed of only lowercase letters."
  },
